WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:06.400
What’s up guys?

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.100
Jared again.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:10.620
Today, we’re finally addressing a movie that
has plagued our comments section for what

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.840
seems like forever: Donnie Darko.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.340
Over the years, we’ve tried to tackle this
head-scratching movie from almost every angle.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:22.730
We wrote a full script for Earthling Cinema
and another for a Philosophy Of video – but

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:24.079
nothing ever panned out.

00:00:24.079 --> 00:00:29.339
Donnie Darko became a Sisyphean task; we did
the research, wrote the scripts, to only delete

00:00:29.339 --> 00:00:30.880
it all and move on.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.480
But we couldn’t just give up.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.740
This movie is a cultural phenomenon.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.020
One might even say it inspired a whole generation
to appreciate independent film.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:43.360
So it behooves us to ask: why did this film
resonate on the level that it did?

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.470
Well, with the help of our new format – Half-Baked
– we think we can finally lay this movie

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:48.470
to rest.

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:53.340
So join me on this slightly confusing, literal
journey through time in this Wisecrack Half-Baked

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.170
Edition: Donnie Darko.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:01:13.000
And yeah, the film’s almost 20 years old,
but hey, spoilers ahead.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:43.069
First, a 
quick recap.

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:48.180
Donnie Darko takes place in 1988, Middlesex
Virginia – a quiet upper class suburb.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.509
Donnie, a high schooler, is woken up by a
mysterious voice in the middle of the night.

00:01:52.509 --> 00:01:55.899
He wanders halfway across town and learns
this:

00:01:55.899 --> 00:01:57.780
“28 days.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.659
6 Hours.

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:01.540
42 minutes.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.420
12 seconds.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:09.060
That…is when the world will end.”

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.549
More worrying still, when Donnie stumbles
back home the next morning, he finds it ruined

00:02:13.549 --> 00:02:17.530
– a jet engine crashed right through his
bedroom.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:21.500
Donnie then spends the rest of the month stumbling
through some weird shit that culminates in

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.310
him saving the world by going back in time.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.799
He does this by, well, uh, we don’t know.

00:02:26.799 --> 00:02:29.560
But it involves listening to this terrifying,
imaginary bunny, Frank.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:32.480
“Did Frank tell you to do these things?”

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:33.580
“I have to obey him.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:34.580
He saved my life.

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:39.700
I have to obey him or I’ll be left all alone.”

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:43.810
In following this supposed master plan, Donnie
does some crazy stuff.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:48.260
He floods his school, burns down a pedophile’s
house, and takes his girlfriend, Gretchen,

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.930
on a Halloween date to an old woman’s cellardoor.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:55.920
But shit goes down and Gretchen is accidentally
run over, leaving Donnie to face doomsday

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:56.920
alone.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:00.750
When the fated day arrives, a wormhole opens
up over Donnie’s house, and he watches from

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:02.659
afar with Gretchen’s body.

00:03:02.659 --> 00:03:05.480
What happens next, well, time travel, I guess.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:09.500
Donnie wakes up in his bed 28 days earlier,
laughing because he’s succeeded in going

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:10.590
back in time.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:15.189
In doing so, he’s saved Gretchen from her
automobile-fated death, and his family – who

00:03:15.189 --> 00:03:17.549
was on a plane going through that wormhole.

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:21.920
Then, right on cue, the jet engine comes crashing
down and kills Donnie.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.720
Meanwhile, the rest of our cast is left feeling
oddly connected, as if somehow vaguely aware

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.620
of Donnie’s sacrifice.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:29.790
Roll credits.

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:34.150
Now if the details left you scratching your
head, don’t worry, you’re not alone.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:39.190
Donnie Darko is such a rapid fire succession
of ideas and themes that it can be a bit difficult

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:40.190
to analyze.

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:42.920
Now is there a grand philosophy for the film?

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:44.490
Well here’s what we came up with.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.799
Idea One: Worldlines and Graham Greene.

00:03:46.799 --> 00:03:51.629
Early on, the film draws parallels between
Donnie’s journey and that of The Destructors,

00:03:51.629 --> 00:03:55.319
a short story by Graham Greene taught in Donnie’s
English class.

00:03:55.319 --> 00:03:59.319
In the story, a gang of London street kids
set their hearts on destroying an old man’s

00:03:59.319 --> 00:04:02.030
home down to the very last brick.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:06.780
They start by dismantling the interior, then
destroy all the valuables and flood the foundation,

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.950
before finally they bring the walls themselves
down.

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:13.450
Surprisingly, though, the boys aren’t doing
this out of hate.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:14.450
As Donny explains:

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:20.319
“Well, they say it right when they flood
the house and tear it to shreds that like

00:04:20.319 --> 00:04:23.940
destruction is a form of creation, so the
fact that they burn the money is ironic.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:27.680
They just want to see what happens when they
tear the world apart.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.930
They want to change things.”

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:36.199
This idea of destruction as an act of creation
is critical to understanding Donnie’s journey.

00:04:36.199 --> 00:04:40.500
After all, Frank’s master plan pretty much
amounts to a bunch of senseless destruction.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.389
When it’s all said and done,Donnie floods
the school, burns down a home, and gets at

00:04:44.389 --> 00:04:45.530
least two people killed.

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:50.720
But the film also heavily implies that everything
Donnie did was absolutely necessary to go

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.800
back in time and save the world.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.880
Now it’s not exactly clear how- as in the
new universe the Paedophile is still at large-

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:01.250
but the director’s cut leads us believe
he was using destruction to create the conditions

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:03.419
upon which the wormhole would appear.

00:05:03.419 --> 00:05:08.320
It does this first by telling us of T., the
short story’s protagonist whose plan seemed

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:09.320
fated.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:13.440
“It was as though this plan had been with
him throughout his life, pondered through

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:19.330
the seasons, now in his fifteenth year crystalized
with the pain of puberty.”

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:24.430
Then it draws a parallel between T and Donnie
by showing us this surreal moment of the film.

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:26.971
“I’m gonna get a beer.”

00:05:26.971 --> 00:05:32.410
Yeah, as Donnie later figures out with the
help of his science teacher, these weird bubble-path-things

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:35.139
were actually a visualization of the future.

00:05:35.139 --> 00:05:42.163
“Well each vessel travels along a vector
of spacetime along its center of gravity.”

00:05:42.163 --> 00:05:43.163
“Like a spear.”

00:05:43.163 --> 00:05:46.020
“I beg your pardon?”

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.620
“Like a spear that comes out of your chest.”

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:51.539
“Um, sure.”

00:05:51.539 --> 00:05:56.800
And this is where the film draws on actual
science These “spears” as Donnie calls

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.520
them are a real concept in physics.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.680
As physicist-philosopher combo David Deutsch
and Michael Lockwood explain: “Your life

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.620
forms a kind of four-dimensional ‘worm’
in space-time: the tip of the worm’s tail

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:12.400
corresponds to the event of your birth, and
the front of its head to the event of your

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:13.400
death.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:20.180
An object, seen at any one instant, is a three-dimensional
cross-section of this long, thin, intricately

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:21.180
curved worm.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:25.600
The line along which the worm lies… is called
the objects worldline.”

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.930
By being able to see his worldline – meaning
his own position in the past, present, and

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:34.860
future – Donnie knows what he needs to do
to make the time machine.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:39.240
Of course, you might be wondering if Donnie
being able to see his future is a logical

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:40.240
contradiction.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:44.460
After all, if the future is written, doesn’t
that mean we have no free will?

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:47.560
And props to the film, it gives a shout out
to this idea:

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:50.400
“Well you’re contradicting yourself, Donny.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:56.010
If we were able to see our destinies manifest
themselves visually, then we’d be given

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.450
the choice to betray our chosen destinies.

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:05.210
And the mere fact that this choice exists
would make all pre-formed destiny come to

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:06.210
an end.”

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:09.500
Now, it’s hard to say that Donnie is contradicting
himself when he says that knowing the future

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:11.660
is compatible with free will.

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:16.060
As philosopher David Lewis explains in his
groundbreaking paper, The Paradoxes of Time

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:22.050
Travel, our definition of possible changes
based on how complete our set of facts is.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:27.080
For example, not knowing my future self, it’s
“possible” that I could mount a bid for

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:29.080
president in 2024.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:33.530
But let’s say I did know the future and
that Bauer 2024 never happened because I’d

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:35.389
rather watch movies and play Red Dead.

00:07:35.389 --> 00:07:40.540
Well, knowing this fact about my future, I
would be right in saying that it’s not possible

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:41.909
I’d run for president.

00:07:41.909 --> 00:07:47.310
Now importantly for Lewis, though, my knowledge
of the future wouldn’t limit my free will

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:48.379
in the present.

00:07:48.379 --> 00:07:53.220
Just because I know I’m more likely to play
video games than run for president, doesn’t

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:57.009
mean the universe would cosmically conspire
to stop me if I tried.

00:07:57.009 --> 00:08:01.050
There won’t be glitches in the Matrix if
I try to mount a bid.

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:05.030
In the end, it’s not that future me couldn’t
run for president; it’s that present me

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:09.379
knows that future me doesn’t run for president
because there’s just too many legendary

00:08:09.379 --> 00:08:10.800
animals to find.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:15.440
All that being said though, does the film
explore the conflict between free will, time

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:16.790
travel and foreknowledge?

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:18.540
Well not really, and that’s why we couldn’t
really make a definitive thesis in our prior

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:19.540
videos.

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:20.830
Not really, and that’s why we couldn’t
really make a definitive thesis in our prior

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:21.830
attempts.

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:25.770
Honestly, it feels like the film is all the
building blocks for some super deep philosophical

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:28.020
insights, but never quite puts them together.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.460
Instead, Donnie talks about changing his worldline
like this:

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:33.940
“Not if you travel in God’s channel.”

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:34.940
“God’s Channel.”

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:35.940
“God’s Channel.”

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:36.940
“God’s Channel.”

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:40.890
While there are a boatload of God fearing
scientists, by citing a transcendental all

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:46.480
powerful being in the argument, the film changes
the topic from the physical to the mystical.

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:50.320
And sure, on the surface this comment might
not make a whole lot of sense,

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:53.980
but, the film is actually tapping into something
much deeper here.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.880
Idea Two: Quantum Physics and the Director’s
Cut.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:01.120
Now “God” might seem like a weird idea
for Donnie to toss around, especially considering

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:06.180
that much of the film is a not so subtle critique
of upper-class conservatism and their sanctimony.

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:12.600
“Nobody cares about responsibility, morality,
family values.”

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:17.520
But that all depends on which version you
watched: the theatrical release or the director’s

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:18.520
cut.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:22.790
While the theatrical cut is a good deal shorter,
it also omits for better or worse a lot of

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:25.170
writer/director Richard Kelly’s worldbuilding.

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:29.180
Particularly, in the Director’s Cut, you
get excerpts like this from Roberta Sparrow’s

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:31.220
book, The Philosophy of Time Travel.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:35.480
Yeah, so to summarize, Donnie’s story takes
place in a “tangent universe” which is

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:39.519
about to blow –that is unless Donnie stops
it in 28 days.

00:09:39.519 --> 00:09:44.390
In setting up Donnie as the Messiah – or
“Living Receiver” in the film’s lingo

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:48.310
the movie gives him some weird powers and
some even weirder eyes.

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:52.709
Donnie then saves the world, returning the
tangent universe to the primary one.

00:09:52.709 --> 00:09:56.690
While that might all seem like some random
stuff that Kelly just made up,

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:59.440
he’s actually drawing on some pretty deep
ideas here.

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:03.550
Afterall, Donnie isn’t just time traveling
– he’s time traveling across parallel

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:04.550
universes.

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:09.440
Which, based on your choice of philosophers
and physicists, is possible.

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:13.310
Physicist Hugh Everett, for example, was famous
for coining the idea of a multiverse as a

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:17.040
way to explain wave function collapse in quantum
mechanics.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:22.839
In quantum mechanics, before being observed,
a particle exists in a superposition of states.

00:10:22.839 --> 00:10:28.470
For instance, a photon acts like both a particle
and a wave until you try to look at it and

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:31.519
then it makes up its mind about being either
one.

00:10:31.519 --> 00:10:37.810
The act of observance somehow forces this
indefinite state to collapse into a definite

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:38.810
state.

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:39.810
How?

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:43.380
Well, there’s no real answer right now,
but Everett theorized that these collapses

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:45.180
don’t really exist at all.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:50.490
Instead, when we observe a particle, we’re
observing a symptom of the universe splitting,

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:53.980
as both states are realized in different realities.

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:58.800
What this means for us is that there are nearly
infinite branching realities running alongside

00:10:58.800 --> 00:10:59.800
our own.

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:04.120
So does that mean a universe could exist that
was cosmically doomed, such as Kelly’s tangent

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:05.120
universe?

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:06.120
Well likely not.

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:08.070
Unlike media’s newfound obsession with multiverses.

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:12.040
“This fry, is your universe, soggy it’s
weird it’s gross.

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:15.000
And this delicious normal fry is my universe.”

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.959
Everett’s theory of the multiple worlds
doesn’t invalidate the basic laws of physics.

00:11:19.959 --> 00:11:22.750
Parallel universes aren’t going to collapse
into each other.

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:26.490
But that doesn’t necessarily rule out Donnie’s
time travel adventures.

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:31.320
“How is that possible?

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:33.410
How is time travel possible?”

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:37.850
Now, I know that might sound weird to say
that time travel is possible, but it lies

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:40.810
within the film’s very specific depiction
of the idea.

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:44.810
Maybe some of you have heard of the grandfather
paradox, which states that backwards time

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:49.530
travel is impossible because than you could
kill your grandfather and then you wouldn’t

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:52.360
exist so then who goes back and kills your
grandfather?.

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:56.899
By invoking parallel universes, Kelly completely
side steps this logical problem.

00:11:56.899 --> 00:12:01.510
Killing your grandfather would just make a
parallel universe where you don’t exist.

00:12:01.510 --> 00:12:05.880
Like the grandfather paradox, how could a
bullet-holed Frank be able to warn Donnie

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:09.610
about the world ending if Donnie went back
in time and saved it?

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:13.670
Well, if Frank was from a parallel universe,
then it all checks out.

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:17.460
And if you think we’re reaching here to
explain away some inconsistencies in the film,

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:18.639
I don’t think so.

00:12:18.639 --> 00:12:21.389
Remember those wordlines we talked about earlier?

00:12:21.389 --> 00:12:25.770
Physicist Kurt Gödel proved that a universe
could hypothetically exist in which these

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:28.290
wordlines were actually closed loops.

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:32.180
That means by going forward in time, you could
end up in your own past.

00:12:32.180 --> 00:12:35.779
Kind of like your future self lapping your
past self in Mario Kart.

00:12:35.779 --> 00:12:40.140
And while our particular universe is not constructed
this way, various physicists have proposed

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:45.360
how these “closed timelike curves” could
theoretically exist, like physicist John Wheeler

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:51.550
and Kip Thorne, who believe these mariokart
track timelines could be generated using wormholes.

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:54.970
And again, Donnie Darko does throw this idea
out there.

00:12:54.970 --> 00:13:00.470
“According to Hawking, a wormhole may be
able to provide a shortcut to jumping between

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:02.449
two distant regions of spacetime.”

00:13:02.449 --> 00:13:07.420
But while the movie alludes to all these weighty
ideas, in the end, everything comes back to

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:09.220
Kelly’s weird mythos.

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:13.830
Sure, Donnie and his teacher talk about science
but then Kelly explains everything away like

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:19.010
this in the Director’s Cut: “Water and
Metal are the key elements of Time Travel.

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:24.060
Water is the barrier element for the construction
of Time Portals used as gateways between Universes

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:25.470
at the Tangent Vortex.”

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:31.630
So, no science, no real discernible philosophy
or belief system, just Kelly’s unique brand

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:32.890
of mysticism.

00:13:32.890 --> 00:13:36.779
And yeah, certainly not enough to really flesh
a Philosophy Of video.

00:13:36.779 --> 00:13:38.620
Idea 3:The Burbs.

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:44.060
So here’s the big question, Wisecrack: Why
is this film such a cultural milestone?

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:46.700
Why do people point to it as a really profound
movie?

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:50.360
While I can point to a lot of things, like
the great cast and the amazing soundtrack,

00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:55.080
for me, it comes down to how well Donnie Darko
functions as an exploration of upper middle-class

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:56.110
angst.

00:13:56.110 --> 00:14:01.250
From its depiction of teenage sexual frustration,
to baby boomers struggling to understand their

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:06.050
child’s mental illness, the film is an incredible
exploration of growing up privileged in the

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:07.130
late 80s.

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:11.390
If any of you have ever lived in suburbia,
you know how frustratingly appearance-focused

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:12.390
it can be.

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:16.580
Take Mrs. Farmer, for example, a parent and
teacher who’s obsessed with her own moral

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:17.580
crusade.

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:21.089
“I want to know why this filth is being
taught to our children.”

00:14:21.089 --> 00:14:25.950
Throughout the film, we see Mrs. Farmer constantly
judge and belittle others for not subscribing

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:30.860
to Jim Cunningham’s BS self-help advice,
especially Donnie’s more liberal mother.

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:33.620
“Our paths through life must be righteous.

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:38.570
I urge you to go home and look in the mirror
and pray that your son doesn’t succumb to

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:39.570
the path of fear.”

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:43.700
Of course, Mrs. Farmer is never concerned
about being truly moral.

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:48.710
Despite her idol, Jim Cunningham, becoming
a convicted pedophile, Mrs. Farmer still holds

00:14:48.710 --> 00:14:51.750
onto her steadfast belief in his moral system.

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:56.560
Proving how righteous she really is, Mrs.
Farmer insists on going to Jim’s trial instead

00:14:56.560 --> 00:14:59.110
of taking her own daughter to a televised
talent show

00:14:59.110 --> 00:15:03.089
– and then shamelessly guilt trips Donnie’s
mother, Rose.

00:15:03.089 --> 00:15:08.170
“Rose, I have to appear at his arraignment
tomorrow morning, and as you know, the girls

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:10.779
are scheduled to leave for Los Angeles in
the morning.

00:15:10.779 --> 00:15:15.860
And as their coach, I was the obvious choice
to chaperone them on their trip, but-”

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:18.230
“But now, you can’t go.”

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:21.130
And this is where the film really succeeds.

00:15:21.130 --> 00:15:26.560
It shows us in stunning detail all the annoying
trappings of middle-class suburban life, and

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:30.200
then gives us a voice in Donnie to speak out
against it.

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:33.660
After all, there’s something viscerally
relatable to Donnie standing up to all the

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:36.339
ridiculous expectations placed on him.

00:15:36.339 --> 00:15:41.459
We’ve all wanted to stand up and say no,
like Donnie did when he rejected Jim Cunningham’s

00:15:41.459 --> 00:15:42.769
ridiculous moral system.

00:15:42.769 --> 00:15:47.920
“You can’t just lump everything into these
two categories then deny everything else.”

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:52.649
And to that end, Donnie’s acts of rebellion
and even destruction feel kind of acceptable.

00:15:52.649 --> 00:15:56.860
He floods the school, sure, but that’s the
same school that fired his favorite teacher

00:15:56.860 --> 00:15:59.110
for daring them to think for themselves.

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:03.480
“I don’t think you have a clue of what
it’s like trying to communicate with these

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:04.480
kids.

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:11.670
We are losing them to apathy – to this prescribed
nonsense.”

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:15.540
He burns down a house, but hey, the owner
was our aforementioned pederast.

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:17.250
“Eight year olds dude.”

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:22.290
In the end, these acts of rebellion function
as a form of escapism to us, the viewers.

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:27.230
As Freud once paraphrased fellow psychologist
Theodor Fontane, people “cannot subsist

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:30.600
on the scanty satisfaction they can extort
from reality.

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:33.990
We simply cannot do without auxiliary constructions”.

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:39.449
In other words, fantasy and wish fulfillment
are necessary to keep us happy and functioning.

00:16:39.449 --> 00:16:44.660
And to that end, Richard Kelly’s weird world
building complete with super powers and time

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:49.240
travel acts as the ultimate form of escapism
from suburban monotony

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:53.209
What if, like Donnie, you actually were the
center of a tangent universe and it was up

00:16:53.209 --> 00:16:54.400
to you to save it?

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:58.630
For me, those thoughts would at least make
all the white picket fences a little bit more

00:16:58.630 --> 00:16:59.630
bearable.

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:00.630
So what do you think, Wisecrack?

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:04.350
Did we spend the last few years missing an
obvious angle Donnie Darko?

00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:08.420
Is there a more philosophically deep and consistent
thesis that we’ve missed?

00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:12.850
Or are you in our camp, and think that what
makes the film great is how it channels our

00:17:12.850 --> 00:17:14.690
middle-class, edgelord angst?

00:17:14.690 --> 00:17:15.870
Drop us a line and let us know in the comments
section.

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:19.160
And if you want to build your own website
for free, check out wix.com/wisecrack.

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:21.690
Peace guys.

